name o4-02-bvr-offset-1
timeout 600

# CAMPAIGN O4, SORTIE 2 OF 10 — the same block, the same two airframes, ONE geometry line different per
# side: both entries are turned 25 degrees off the line between them, so the tracks cross at 50 degrees.
# doc/campaigns/o4-gaf-mig29g-dact.md mission 2.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: what does a crossing target cost each radar? The two answers are not the
# same kind of thing, and that is the point. The APG-68 keeps the track and spends the ROUND's energy on
# the turn (doc/duels.md row 2: two AIM-120s expiring 25.5 m and 19.4 m out). The N019 loses the track
# itself: one look through a +-6 degree elevation bar is all a walking depression angle allows, and the
# 6 s inertial coast expires before the next 3.0 s frame reaches the target. A crossing geometry is a
# SENSOR problem for the MiG and a KINEMATIC problem for the F-16.
#
# WHAT THE CAMPAIGN ADDS TO doc/duels.md's own `duel-offset`: this sortie is flown with what sortie 1
# left on the racks. The campaign carries `stores`, so every `set store` line below is a REQUEST — the
# ones the book cannot cover are dropped before the spawn and appear as `campaign CARRY` lines in this
# step's events.log. Run standalone the jet has six rounds; run in the campaign it has what it landed
# with. Both numbers are reported.
#
# MEASURED (2026-07-29, as campaign step 2, entering with 2 of 4 AIM-120 and 1 of 2 R-27R — three
# `campaign CARRY` lines): a draw, exit 3. The AIM-120 arrives 6.57 m out (a crossing target spends the
# round's energy on turning) and the R-27R 11.96 m against its own 13.8 m fuze — inside the ring,
# outside the lethal radius. Standalone (six rounds on the rails instead of four) the same file is also
# exit 3, with the same two shots at t=158.3/159.9 and arrivals of 6.42 m and 12.28 m — 0.14 m and
# 0.32 m from the carried run's, which is the two dropped rails' mass and drag and nothing else. On THIS
# geometry the carry costs the F-16 two rounds and no result: the honest shape of a carry that has not
# bitten yet. It bites in sortie 3.
#
# READING RULE: exit 3 (TIMEOUT) is the expected answer STANDALONE, and it is a draw for opposite reasons
# on the two sides. Inside the campaign the answer may differ and the difference IS the carry — read the
# `campaign CARRY` lines first, then eng_shots / eng_lock_s / eng_detect_s. The MiG's radar behaviour is
# read off its RADAR_CONTACT / RADAR_DROP lines and its coastS, never off the exit code.

unit viper
  team friendly
  module f16
  spawn 55.20000 12.72600 8000 115.0 450      # 25 deg south of the line to the MiG
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm
  set fcr_range_nm 40
  set rwr on
  set store 1 aim120
  set store 2 aim120
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 4 aim120
  set store 6 aim9
  set store 7 aim9
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  wp 55.20000 14.60000 8000 450
  objective kill unit fulcrum
  objective survive

unit fulcrum
  team hostile
  module mig29
  spawn 55.20000 14.47400 8000 245.0 450      # 25 deg south of the line to the F-16: they cross at 50
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_mode rad
  set n019_range_nm 27
  set n019_emission illum
  set rwr on
  set store 3 r27r
  set store 4 r27r
  set store 5 r73
  set store 6 r73
  set store 1 r73
  set store 2 r73
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 30
  set cmds_flare 30
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  wp 55.20000 12.60000 8000 450
  objective kill unit viper
  objective survive

unit hulkwest
  module target_soft
  team friendly
  spawn 55.20000 12.60000 ground 90.0 0

unit hulkeast
  module target_soft
  team hostile
  spawn 55.20000 14.60000 ground 270.0 0
